{"maxId":281,"entries":{"chapter-28-study-anesthesia|Chapter 28 Study: Anesthesia":["u1"],"summary|Summary":["u2"],"copy|Match general, regional/neuraxial or monitored anesthesia care to the procedure, comorbidities and patient preferences (p384).":["u3"],"copy|Older adults are more sensitive to anesthetics and opioids; age-adjust dosing and monitor depth, ventilation, circulation and temperature (p383-385).":["u4"],"copy|Avoid excessive depth and hypotension while preventing awareness; processed EEG may supplement clinical monitoring (p383-385).":["u5"],"copy|In general, keep BP within 20% of baseline and MAP at least 65 mm Hg; older patients, especially with hypertension, may need substantially higher targets (p388).":["u6"],"copy|Regional techniques can improve analgesia and reduce opioid use, but the book finds no conclusive superiority for major postoperative outcomes (p388).":["u7"],"copy|Anticoagulants require coordinated planning for neuraxial placement, catheter removal and medication resumption (p388).":["u8"],"copy|Delirium and longer-term cognitive dysfunction can coexist but are distinct; their relationship and anesthetic causation remain uncertain (p390-391).":["u9"],"copy|Prevention emphasizes orientation, mobilization, sleep, sensory aids and adequate pain control; no preventive pharmaceutical is recommended in the chapter (p384, p396).":["u10"],"copy|For monitored sedation, the book discusses reduced doses of propofol, ketamine or dexmedetomidine and avoidance of benzodiazepines; continuously monitor breathing because light sedation can deepen rapidly (p388).":["u11"],"copy|Review anticholinergic and other high-risk drugs; dose details and agent-specific cautions are in the source tables (p386-387, p392).":["u12"],"copy|Cardiac operations have procedure-specific hemodynamic and monitoring needs; off-pump benefits remain uncertain overall, with possible benefit in geriatric subgroup analyses (p384, p392-393).":["u13"],"copy|TAVR may use sedation or general anesthesia; inability to lie still/flat, imaging needs or complications may require conversion (p393-394).":["u14"],"copy|Clarify the device indication and whether the patient is pacemaker-dependent. Plan for ICD interference and rescue during the procedure (p396).":["u15"],"copy|Do not automatically suspend an Allow Natural Death order. Discuss and document acceptable interventions, goals and the period of any modification with the patient and perioperative team (p395-396).":["u16"],"why-the-same-anesthetic-dose-can-have-a-different-effect|Why the same anesthetic dose can have a different effect":["u275"],"what-changes-with-the-route-of-anesthesia|What changes with the route of anesthesia":["u276"],"designing-the-anesthetic-around-the-patient-and-procedure|Designing the anesthetic around the patient and procedure":["u277"],"intravenous-agents-relate-the-adverse-effect-to-organ-reserve|Intravenous agents: relate the adverse effect to organ reserve":["u278"],"residual-weakness-and-respiratory-recovery|Residual weakness and respiratory recovery":["u279"],"following-perfusion-ventilation-and-temperature|Following perfusion, ventilation and temperature":["u280"],"counselling-about-postoperative-outcomes|Counselling about postoperative outcomes":["u281"],"constructing-a-prevention-and-recovery-plan|Constructing a prevention and recovery plan":["u24"],"cardiac-procedures-why-the-anesthetic-decision-changes-with-the-anatomy|Cardiac procedures: why the anesthetic decision changes with the anatomy":["u25"],"pacemakers-defibrillators-and-acceptable-rescue|Pacemakers, defibrillators and acceptable rescue":["u26"],"exam-traps|Exam traps":["u27"],"copy|A broader or combined anesthetic block may reduce analgesic requirements without guaranteeing protection from delirium (p388–391).":["u28"],"copy|Oxygen saturation can remain reassuring during hypoventilation on supplemental oxygen. Observe airflow and ventilation as well (p388–389).":["u29"],"copy|A patient moving a limb after anesthesia can still have residual pharyngeal weakness (p385–387).":["u30"],"copy|A shorter incision in off-pump bypass does not establish less pain after thoracotomy (p393).":["u31"],"copy|Do not infer pacemaker dependence solely from a high percentage of ventricular pacing in a resynchronization device (p396).":["u32"],"copy|Limits on postoperative life support may differ from the patient's wishes for a brief, reversible intraoperative complication (p395–396).":["u33"],"copy|Spontaneous breathing during sedation does not ensure adequate ventilation; monitor the patient throughout (p388).":["u34"],"copy|The chapter's general blood-pressure floor needs interpretation relative to baseline, especially in chronic hypertension (p388).":["u35"],"copy|An established limit on resuscitation should prompt a perioperative discussion of particular interventions and timing (p395–396).":["u36"],"past-exam-questions|Past exam questions":["u37"],"recall-drill|Recall drill":["u38"],"copy|CHAPTER 28 / HAZZARD":["u18"],"copy|Study summary of Chapter 28; the book and the full chapter are the reference.":["u19"],"copy|Choose and monitor anesthesia":["u20"],"copy|Cognition and medicines":["u21"],"copy|Cardiac procedures and goals":["u22"],"copy|An older adult may have a greater and later hemodynamic response to an induction dose.":["u44"],"copy|Table 28-1 advises smaller, incremental doses with time to assess effect.":["u45"],"copy|Propofol can cause hypotension.":["u46"],"copy|Etomidate depresses cortisol production; the chapter flags sepsis as a concern.":["u47"],"copy|Morphine can depress respiration and its active glucuronide metabolite is cleared less effectively in renal failure.":["u48"],"copy|The table gives agent-specific cautions rather than one dose for every patient (p385–387).":["u49"],"copy|During general anesthesia, use ventilation, oxygenation, circulatory and temperature monitoring.":["u50"],"copy|A nerve stimulator helps assess neuromuscular blockade and its reversal.":["u51"],"copy|Even a small residual block can impair pharyngeal muscles and raise aspiration risk.":["u52"],"copy|During monitored anesthesia care the patient may still breathe spontaneously, but supplemental oxygen does not replace assessment of respiratory rate and end-tidal carbon dioxide (p385, p388).":["u53"],"copy|A peripheral block acts around a nerve.":["u54"],"copy|A spinal block is subarachnoid, whereas an epidural can be maintained through a catheter.":["u55"],"copy|Regional techniques may spare opioids and provide continuing analgesia.":["u56"],"copy|For anticoagulated patients, the question includes timing of catheter removal and medication restart, drug clearance and the consequences of bleeding into a noncompressible space.":["u57"],"copy|Modality alone does not reliably predict major postoperative outcomes (p388).":["u58"],"copy|The term perioperative neurocognitive disorders spans pre-existing cognitive illness and new postoperative syndromes.":["u59"],"copy|In the terminology summarized here, delayed neurocognitive recovery covers decline up to 30 days, while postoperative neurocognitive disorder describes decline persisting or identified up to 12 months.":["u60"],"copy|Acute postoperative delirium has an acute, fluctuating course and needs prompt evaluation (p389–390).":["u61"],"copy|Describe what the operation requires before comparing techniques.":["u62"],"copy|Will the patient need to remain immobile for a prolonged period?":["u63"],"copy|Must the team control ventilation or obtain transesophageal images?":["u64"],"copy|How much postoperative pain is expected, and which interventions would the patient accept if a complication occurred?":["u65"],"copy|Then ask how the person functioned before this illness.":["u66"],"copy|Baseline cognition, hearing, mobility and ability to lie flat affect both the anesthetic plan and the interpretation of postoperative changes.":["u67"],"copy|Geriatric surgical programs bring this information into the operative handoff so that a patient who is normally attentive and independent is recognized when they return to the ward quiet and inattentive (p383–385, p389–392).":["u68"],"copy|Figure 28-1 distinguishes monitored anesthesia care, regional anesthesia, general anesthesia and a combined regional/general approach.":["u69"],"copy|General anesthesia may be maintained with an inhaled agent such as isoflurane or sevoflurane, with an intravenous technique, or with a combination.":["u70"],"copy|Regional techniques include spinal and epidural blockade, peripheral nerve blocks for a limb and blocks for the trunk.":["u71"],"copy|A block can be part of a general anesthetic or provide continuing postoperative analgesia.":["u72"],"copy|Knowing the name of a technique is not enough to predict how sedated the patient was, what the airway required or how much postoperative opioid they will need (p384–385, p388).":["u73"],"copy|Table 28-1 emphasizes that the initial distribution of hypnotic drugs changes with age and clearance of some hepatically extracted drugs falls as liver blood flow decreases.":["u74"],"copy|A hemodynamic effect may be delayed.":["u75"],"copy|Repeating a dose before observing its full effect can overshoot the intended depth.":["u76"],"copy|The table consequently describes reduced, incremental induction dosing for older patients.":["u77"],"copy|It does not give a fixed milligram dose for each hypnotic.":["u78"],"copy|Compare agents by the feature relevant to this patient's circulation, respiration and postoperative cognition rather than by onset speed alone (p385–386).":["u79"],"copy|Propofol permits rapid induction and maintenance and has antiemetic properties, but hypotension is important when perfusion reserve is limited.":["u80"],"copy|Etomidate is discussed for patients with impaired ventricular function, with cortisol suppression a concern in sepsis; involuntary movements, nausea and vomiting may occur.":["u81"],"copy|Ketamine can provide analgesia and dissociative anesthesia but produces sympathetic effects, secretions and sometimes distressing emergence phenomena.":["u82"],"copy|Methohexital has a role in electroconvulsive therapy and can produce excitatory movements.":["u83"],"copy|Thiopental can provoke cough or laryngospasm.":["u84"],"copy|Midazolam provides anxiolysis and amnesia, but the authors caution about delirium in older adults and identify flumazenil as short acting.":["u85"],"copy|An apparent recovery after reversal still requires observation (p386).":["u86"],"copy|Opioids differ in kinetics while sharing respiratory and central nervous system effects.":["u87"],"copy|Morphine can produce histamine-related effects, constipation and urinary retention, and its active glucuronide metabolite can persist with renal failure.":["u88"],"copy|Fentanyl has rapid onset and can cause chest-wall rigidity at high doses.":["u89"],"copy|Sufentanil also depresses respiration.":["u90"],"copy|Remifentanil is ultra-short acting and can be titrated by infusion.":["u91"],"copy|Alfentanil is short acting, but coadministration of erythromycin reduces its clearance.":["u92"],"copy|The book gives a class-level suggestion to reduce opioid dosing by 50% in patients aged 80 or older and titrate to effect.":["u93"],"copy|Apply this as the chapter's monitored perioperative principle, not an equipotency rule between drugs or routes.":["u94"],"copy|Naloxone reverses opioid effects (p386–387).":["u95"],"copy|Neuromuscular blockade improves surgical conditions but is distinct from hypnosis, amnesia and analgesia.":["u96"],"copy|Consider both the intended intraoperative effect and recovery of the muscles needed to keep the upper airway open, swallow and cough.":["u97"],"copy|Older adults can be clinically vulnerable to residual pharyngeal weakness even after visible limb movement returns.":["u98"],"copy|Determine the depth of block, allow for prolonged action when clearance is reduced, and check recovery after reversal rather than relying solely on elapsed time (p385–388).":["u99"],"copy|Pancuronium is long acting, with 30%–70% excreted unchanged in urine according to Table 28-1.":["u100"],"copy|Renal impairment can prolong its action; the table also notes tachycardia.":["u101"],"copy|The intermediate-acting nondepolarizers include rocuronium and vecuronium.":["u102"],"copy|Neuromuscular disease can markedly increase sensitivity.":["u103"],"copy|Cisatracurium is highlighted for its limited dependence on organ clearance and comparatively preserved recovery with aging.":["u104"],"copy|Succinylcholine is a short-acting depolarizing agent with a different role.":["u105"],"copy|Neostigmine or sugammadex is listed for reversal of vecuronium or rocuronium.":["u106"],"copy|The chapter's practical endpoint is recovery adequate for the planned airway management, not the mere fact that a reversal agent has been given (p387).":["u107"],"copy|Current practice (not the book): For patients receiving neuromuscular blockers, ASA recommends quantitative monitoring at the adductor pollicis and confirmation of a train-of-four ratio of at least 0.9 before extubation. - ASA monitoring and antagonism practice guideline, 2023.":["u108"],"copy|An older person with longstanding hypertension may lose adequate cerebral or coronary perfusion at a pressure that looks acceptable in isolation.":["u109"],"copy|The authors use a general target within 20% of baseline pressure and a mean arterial pressure of at least 65 mm Hg, with higher individual targets sometimes needed.":["u110"],"copy|Studies cited associate even relatively brief hypotension with adverse outcomes; the depth and duration of the change matter.":["u111"],"copy|Interpret a low reading in relation to the baseline, bleeding, anesthetic depth, vascular tone, rhythm and ventricular function rather than assuming a single intervention fits each cause (p388).":["u112"],"copy|Pulse oximetry answers a different question from capnography.":["u113"],"copy|Supplemental oxygen can maintain saturation during impaired ventilation.":["u114"],"copy|Observe the respiratory pattern and airway and use end-tidal carbon dioxide monitoring as appropriate, including during sedation outside the operating room.":["u115"],"copy|With age-related sensitivity, a patient who began a procedure breathing comfortably can become obstructed or hypoventilate as additional medication takes effect.":["u116"],"copy|Temperature deserves similar attention: active warming and maintaining normothermia are part of protecting an older patient from perioperative stress.":["u117"],"copy|Communicate episodes of hypotension, hypoxia or delayed recovery to the postoperative team (p385, p388–389, p391–392).":["u118"],"copy|The level of block and the timing of anticoagulant therapy affect regional choices.":["u119"],"copy|A peripheral technique can reduce systemic analgesic requirements and an epidural may provide ongoing relief through a catheter.":["u120"],"copy|Bleeding into a confined space can have serious consequences.":["u121"],"copy|Consider not only placement but catheter removal, timing of drug resumption, formulation and the patient's kidney function.":["u122"],"copy|Anticoagulant clearance may be prolonged in an older patient.":["u123"],"copy|The chapter does not offer one safe interval applicable to all antithrombotic agents and procedures (p388).":["u124"],"copy|Table 28-2 gives approximate ranges for older surgical patients.":["u125"],"copy|Delirium is listed at 12%–24% and cognitive dysfunction at 10%–16%, compared with 2%–4% for pneumonia, 0.4%–2% for myocardial infarction and 0.3%–1% for stroke.":["u126"],"copy|The other estimates are 0.2%–0.5% for pulmonary embolism, 0.4%–0.6% for acute renal failure, 0.6%–6% for heart failure and 2%–5% for death.":["u127"],"copy|These outcomes differ in definition and can overlap.":["u128"],"copy|Adding the percentages is not a way to estimate the risk for an individual facing a particular operation (p389).":["u129"],"copy|Ask about pre-existing changes in concentration, learning and daily function before surgery.":["u130"],"copy|Postoperative delirium develops acutely, often fluctuates and requires a search for precipitating causes.":["u131"],"copy|Delayed neurocognitive recovery and postoperative neurocognitive disorder describe cognitive changes assessed on longer time scales.":["u132"],"copy|An older person can have both cognitive impairment at baseline and a superimposed postoperative episode.":["u133"],"copy|Without baseline information it is difficult to separate a pre-existing limitation from a new decline.":["u134"],"copy|Tell the recovery team what the patient could do before admission and what constitutes a meaningful change to them (p389–391).":["u135"],"copy|Studies asking whether a particular technique prevents delirium must be read with their comparators.":["u136"],"copy|In ENGAGES, older adults undergoing major surgery received EEG-guided anesthesia or usual care.":["u137"],"copy|The book reports delirium in 26% versus 23%, respectively, without a significant benefit from the guided strategy.":["u138"],"copy|Other studies the chapter discusses raised the possibility of benefit from avoiding excessive depth.":["u139"],"copy|A monitor can inform titration and reveal a cerebral response to anesthetic without guaranteeing prevention of delirium.":["u140"],"copy|Similarly, regional anesthesia and general anesthesia are broad labels: sedation depth, underlying risk and postoperative care can differ within either arm of a comparison (p389–391).":["u141"],"copy|In Table 28-3 the panels agree on preoperative cognitive screening.":["u142"],"copy|Several support multicomponent care and avoiding high-risk medicines.":["u143"],"copy|Their recommendations are less consistent for processed EEG monitoring, regional anesthesia and cerebral perfusion measures.":["u144"],"copy|Some entries indicate no recommendation or an inability to recommend a strategy.":["u145"],"copy|This represents an evidence limitation, not evidence that every useful component must succeed as a stand-alone intervention.":["u146"],"copy|Maintain hearing and vision aids, protect sleep, support orientation, mobilize where feasible and address pain, oxygenation and other physiological disturbances (p390–392).":["u147"],"copy|Pain itself can impede sleep, breathing and mobility; excessive sedative or opioid exposure can also impair attention and ventilation.":["u148"],"copy|Review what the patient actually received in theatre, recovery and on the ward before adding another agent.":["u149"],"copy|Table 28-4 includes diclofenac, ibuprofen, etodolac, meloxicam and naproxen among NSAIDs with gastrointestinal bleeding concerns, particularly at age 75 or older or with anticoagulants, antiplatelets or corticosteroids.":["u150"],"copy|Ketorolac adds renal and gastrointestinal concerns, while indomethacin has particular CNS adverse effects.":["u151"],"copy|For postoperative nausea and vomiting, distinguish the proposed agent's antiemetic benefit from its cognitive and motor effects.":["u153"],"copy|The table lists ondansetron 4 mg IV every 6 hours, haloperidol 0.5–1 mg every 6 hours and metoclopramide 5 mg IV once as examples, along with propofol and aprepitant for selected circumstances.":["u154"],"copy|These are the printed table entries, not an instruction to combine or repeat all of them.":["u155"],"copy|The same table urges caution with antipsychotics and steroids, and lists dexamethasone, particularly above 4 mg, among concerns for vulnerable patients.":["u156"],"copy|Diphenhydramine, hydroxyzine, scopolamine, promethazine, prochlorperazine and tricyclic agents can add central anticholinergic effects and oversedation.":["u157"],"copy|Review the entire exposure when an older patient becomes inattentive (p392).":["u158"],"copy|Coronary disease is common in older surgical patients, but a diagnosis of coronary disease is not the same as evidence that every patient needs the same invasive monitoring.":["u159"],"copy|The chapter describes using an arterial line where rapid pressure changes or limited reserve would make intermittent readings inadequate.":["u160"],"copy|The question is what information a measurement adds and what the team could change in response.":["u161"],"copy|During coronary procedures consider the balance between myocardial oxygen supply and demand, baseline ventricular function, rhythm and the hemodynamic consequences of anesthesia or manipulation of the heart (p392–393).":["u162"],"copy|Conventional coronary bypass uses cardiopulmonary bypass.":["u163"],"copy|Off-pump surgery avoids the pump but has its own technical and hemodynamic demands.":["u164"],"copy|A minimally invasive thoracotomy uses a smaller incision; the authors caution that pain can nevertheless be greater than with sternotomy.":["u165"],"copy|The chapter does not establish a general superiority of off-pump surgery for all patients.":["u166"],"copy|Subgroup findings in older people and an individual patient's coronary anatomy, comorbidity and surgical expertise can inform the discussion.":["u167"],"copy|Percutaneous coronary intervention avoids some surgical stress but still requires a plan for cooperation, access complications and cardiovascular instability (p392–393).":["u168"],"copy|Degenerative aortic stenosis is common in later life.":["u169"],"copy|TAVR can be performed with monitored sedation or general anesthesia.":["u170"],"copy|A patient who cannot lie flat, tolerate imaging or remain still may require a different plan from one who can cooperate comfortably.":["u171"],"copy|Access problems, bleeding or cardiac instability can require conversion during a planned sedated procedure.":["u172"],"copy|The chapter cites complication rates up to 20% in its TAVR material, including approximately 2% stroke, 3.6% significant postprocedural leak and 6% major bleeding.":["u173"],"copy|Mitral regurgitation can reflect primary valve disease or an effect of ventricular dysfunction.":["u175"],"copy|The chapter contrasts surgical repair or replacement with catheter approaches in selected patients.":["u176"],"copy|Imaging can be central to assessing the lesion and the result of repair; the need for transesophageal imaging and patient immobility affects the anesthetic plan.":["u177"],"copy|During atrial fibrillation ablation, discomfort, procedure length, movement and airway obstruction may make sedation inadequate.":["u178"],"copy|Evaluate the patient's ability to lie still, antithrombotic therapy and baseline rhythm.":["u179"],"copy|Device implantation and electrophysiologic procedures may be less invasive than open surgery while still requiring readiness for sudden cardiovascular or respiratory events (p394–395).":["u180"],"copy|Identify why the cardiac device was implanted, what it is programmed to do and whether the patient depends on pacing.":["u181"],"copy|A high proportion of ventricular pacing in a resynchronization device can be expected rather than proof of malfunction.":["u182"],"copy|Electromagnetic interference and the potential for an inappropriate shock matter during surgery.":["u183"],"copy|Obtain a device-specific perioperative plan, clarify how rhythm will be monitored and how pacing or defibrillation would be provided if needed, and arrange restoration of the intended postoperative settings.":["u184"],"copy|Distinguish a discussion about disabling shocks from one about ongoing pacing support (p395–396).":["u185"],"copy|A pre-existing order limiting resuscitation deserves a conversation before anesthesia.":["u186"],"copy|Airway support, a vasoactive medicine or a brief reversible rhythm disturbance in theatre may not mean the same thing to the patient as prolonged ventilation or intensive care.":["u187"],"copy|Explain the likely perioperative circumstances, identify treatments the patient accepts or declines, agree when any modification ends, document it and communicate it to the recovery team.":["u188"],"copy|If capacity is impaired, involve the appropriate decision-maker and use the patient's known priorities.":["u189"],"copy|In a US study cited, 32% underwent inpatient surgery in the last year of life, 18% in the last month and 8% in the last week.":["u190"],"copy|That frequency makes a specific perioperative conversation relevant even for an apparently routine procedure (p395–396).":["u191"],"copy|One hemodynamic consequence of correcting mitral regurgitation deserves special attention.":["u192"],"copy|Before repair, part of the ventricular stroke volume can leave through the regurgitant valve.":["u193"],"copy|After repair the effective load against which the left ventricle ejects may increase substantially.":["u194"],"copy|Preoperative ventricular function and the ability to tolerate that change are therefore relevant to patient selection and postoperative support.":["u195"],"copy|The operative mortality estimate below 2% cited in the chapter came from patients whose average age was 60.":["u196"],"copy|Using that figure as an individual estimate for a frail, much older person would discard the population on which it was based (p394).":["u197"],"copy|The patient's experience of an implanted defibrillator can also influence preparation.":["u198"],"copy|The book describes distress related to previous or feared shocks.":["u199"],"copy|Explain who will monitor the rhythm and what support is available if device therapies are temporarily changed.":["u200"],"copy|Review the plan for electrocautery with the procedural team and confirm the postoperative handoff.":["u201"],"copy|This discussion can reduce anxiety and reveal a preference about future shocks that the usual device interrogation cannot answer.":["u202"],"copy|Incorporate that preference into the wider goals-of-care conversation rather than relying on the device's default programming (p395–396).":["u203"],"copy|No past exam questions are mapped to this chapter in the 2020–2026 reference list.":["u204"],"copy|1.":["u205"],"copy|Which factors determine the choice between general, regional and monitored anesthesia?":["u206"],"copy|2.":["u207"],"copy|Why should induction drugs be given slowly in smaller increments?":["u208"],"copy|3.":["u209"],"copy|What is a major concern with propofol induction in an older patient?":["u210"],"copy|4.":["u211"],"copy|Why does kidney function affect the choice or dosing of morphine?":["u212"],"copy|5.":["u213"],"copy|Why verify recovery from a neuromuscular block before extubation?":["u214"],"copy|6.":["u215"],"copy|What can processed EEG add to routine anesthetic monitoring?":["u216"],"copy|7.":["u217"],"copy|What general BP targets does the book describe?":["u218"],"copy|8.":["u219"],"copy|How do spinal and epidural blocks differ anatomically?":["u220"],"copy|9.":["u221"],"copy|Which decisions require coordination for an anticoagulated patient receiving a neuraxial catheter?":["u222"],"copy|10.":["u223"],"copy|What must be followed in a spontaneously breathing patient receiving monitored anesthesia care?":["u224"],"copy|11.":["u225"],"copy|What advantage of a regional technique is well supported in the chapter?":["u226"],"copy|12.":["u227"],"copy|Which postoperative syndromes use the 30-day and 12-month windows?":["u228"],"copy|13.":["u229"],"copy|Which preventive measures matter for postoperative delirium?":["u230"],"copy|14.":["u231"],"copy|What may necessitate general anesthesia or conversion during TAVR?":["u232"],"copy|15.":["u233"],"copy|How should an existing Allow Natural Death order be addressed?":["u234"],"copy|Answer":["u235","u237","u239","u241","u243","u245","u247","u255","u257","u259","u261","u263","u269","u271","u273"],"copy|The procedure, comorbidities, risks and patient or surrogate preferences, discussed with the anesthesia team (p384, p388).":["u236"],"copy|Changes in distribution and clearance increase sensitivity; the hemodynamic response may be accentuated or delayed (p385–386).":["u238"],"copy|Hypotension. Titrate to effect while monitoring circulation (p386).":["u240"],"copy|Its active metabolite morphine-6-glucuronide can accumulate when renal clearance is impaired, increasing risk of prolonged opioid effects (p386).":["u242"],"copy|Residual blockade can impair pharyngeal function and predispose to aspiration (p385).":["u244"],"copy|An additional estimate of depth when usual signals are difficult to interpret, supporting titration while avoiding both excessive anesthesia and awareness (p385).":["u246"],"copy|Within":["u248"],"copy|20%":["u249"],"copy|of baseline, MAP at least":["u250"],"copy|65 mm Hg":["u251"],"copy|and systolic pressure at least":["u252"],"copy|100 mm Hg":["u253"],"copy|. Chronic hypertension may call for higher targets (p388).":["u254"],"copy|A spinal block delivers local anesthetic into the subarachnoid space; an epidural acts in the epidural space (p388).":["u256"],"copy|When to stop medication, place the block, maintain or remove the catheter, and restart therapy in light of agent and clearance (p388).":["u258"],"copy|Respiratory rate, oxygen saturation, end-tidal carbon dioxide and other standard vital signs; light sedation can deepen rapidly (p388).":["u260"],"copy|Analgesia with reduced opioid requirements. Studies cited do not establish broad superiority over general anesthesia for mortality or delirium (p388).":["u262"],"copy|Delayed neurocognitive recovery covers decline up to":["u264"],"copy|30 days":["u265"],"copy|; postoperative neurocognitive disorder covers a persisting or identified decline up to":["u266"],"copy|12 months":["u267"],"copy|(p389).":["u268"],"copy|Multicomponent care including orientation, sleep protection, sensory aids, mobilization and adequate pain treatment (p384, p390, p396).":["u270"],"copy|Inability to cooperate or lie flat, specific imaging needs or a procedural complication can make sedation insufficient (p393–394).":["u272"],"copy|Discuss permissible measures and goals with patient or surrogate and team, and document any modification and when it ends (p395–396).":["u274"],"copy|Gabapentin requires dose reduction with a GFR below 60 in the table and should be avoided in end-stage renal disease; meperidine is particularly problematic in chronic kidney disease because of its metabolite (p392).":["u152"],"copy|The figures describe the experience at the time of the book, not a contemporary risk calculator for a particular device or center (p393–394).":["u174"]},"sequenceOffsets":[[287,5],[290,-139],[292,-136],[294,-7],[299,-100]]}
